{
  "summary": "Reduces the message hash modulo the curve order n before deriving RFC 6979 nonces in the test framework's ECKey signing helper. It also adds a unit test verifying deterministic ECDSA signatures against libsecp256k1 output, covering a hash value greater than n.",
  "problem": "The test framework's `sign_ecdsa(..., rfc6979=True)` derived deterministic nonces using the raw 32-byte message hash without first reducing it modulo n, deviating from RFC 6979 section 2.3.4. While resulting signatures remained valid, they diverged from libsecp256k1 for hashes greater than or equal to n.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author description: \"RFC6979 section 2.3.4 (bits2octets) reduces the hash mod n first. libsecp256k1 has done so since 0.2.0. So for a hash >= n the framework's signature differs from libsecp256k1's, though it is still valid.\"",
    "Author notes practical impact is near zero: \"The change has no practical effect on current tests: the only caller, feature_taproot.py, signs double-SHA256 sighashes, which are >= n with probability about 2^-128.\""
  ],
